<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple Computer//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    The bundle's Info.plist.

    This began as Qt's own Info.plist.app.in plus CFBundleIconName, because Qt
    supplied a template unless one was named here. Nothing supplies one now, so it
    is simply the file this application wants, but it is left as it stands
    rather than trimmed, because every key below has been through a real bundle
    and pruning a plist by guesswork is how a bundle stops launching.

    One substitution pass runs over it, not the two the Qt build needed, and both
    forms go through it: CMake generates the plist with configure_file() in its
    default mode, which expands ${FOO} and @FOO@ alike.

      ${FOO}   the MACOSX_BUNDLE_* target properties.
      @FOO@    variables set in app/CMakeLists.txt before set_target_properties
               runs. This is what makes the icon-name entry conditional: an empty
               value leaves nothing behind, where a key written unconditionally
               would have LaunchServices look up a name that is not there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>

	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_BUNDLE_NAME}</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_GUI_IDENTIFIER}</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_EXECUTABLE_NAME}</string>

	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_BUNDLE_VERSION}</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_SHORT_VERSION_STRING}</string>

	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>${CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET}</string>

	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_COPYRIGHT}</string>

	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>${MACOSX_BUNDLE_ICON_FILE}</string>
@XPCOG_INFO_PLIST_ICON_NAME@
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleAllowMixedLocalizations</key>
	<true/>

	<key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
	<string>NSApplication</string>

	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticGraphicsSwitching</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	    Privacy usage descriptions, in two groups, and the second group is the
	    interesting one.

	    macOS shows the matching string inside the permission alert. A key that
	    is missing is not a permission that is skipped: for the folder ones the
	    alert appears anyway with no explanation, and for several other TCC
	    classes the process is killed outright the moment it touches the API.
	    So the string is not a courtesy. It is the only part of that dialog
	    written by somebody who knows what the program was doing.

	    Group one is what XPCog genuinely reaches for. It never asks for any of
	    these up front; macOS asks the first time a file in that location is
	    opened, which for a player means the moment somebody adds music that
	    happens to live there.

	    Group two is Cog's idea and it is a good one. None of those permissions
	    is anything an audio player has any business holding, and XPCog asks for
	    none of them, so a prompt naming one of them is evidence that something
	    is wrong. The string is there to say so to the person being asked, who
	    is the only one in a position to refuse. Cog phrases them as
	    rhetorical questions ("Why are you trying to open your Calendar with an
	    audio player?"); these say the same thing flatter and end with what to do
	    about it.

	    Three keys Cog carries that are deliberately not here:

	      NSAppTransportSecurity with NSAllowsArbitraryLoads, which Cog needs
	      because it streams over plain HTTP through CFNetwork. ATS polices
	      NSURLSession and nothing else; XPCog's HTTP source is libcurl, which it
	      never sees, so the key would grant an exemption from a policy that does
	      not apply and claim a behaviour this build does not have.

	      NSMotionUsageDescription, for the head-tracking headphones Cog's
	      spatial audio supports. Nothing here reads a motion sensor.

	      NSAppleEventsUsageDescription, and NSAppleScriptEnabled with it.
	      AppleScript is one of the things docs/PORTING.md lists as deliberately
	      not ported, so XPCog neither sends Apple events nor answers them.

	    These strings are English only. Everything a listener reads in the
	    interface goes through the wx catalogue compiled into the binary, and a
	    plist is read by launchd and TCC long before any of that is loaded.
	    Localising it needs InfoPlist.strings files in each .lproj, which is
	    machinery this build does not have. Cog localises its through
	    InfoPlist.xcstrings, which is Xcode's.
	-->

	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog opens the audio files you add to it and scans the folders you add to its library. Allow this if your music, or a playlist that points at it, lives on the Desktop. XPCog reads nothing else there and writes nothing.</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog opens the audio files you add to it and scans the folders you add to its library. Allow this if your music, or a playlist that points at it, lives in Documents. XPCog reads nothing else there and writes nothing.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog opens the audio files you add to it and scans the folders you add to its library. Allow this if your music, or a playlist that points at it, lives in Downloads. XPCog reads nothing else there and writes nothing.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog opens the audio files you add to it and scans the folders you add to its library. Allow this to play from a USB drive, an external disc or a memory card. XPCog reads nothing else on the volume and writes nothing.</string>
	<key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog opens the audio files you add to it and scans the folders you add to its library. Allow this if your collection lives on a NAS or another shared volume. XPCog reads nothing else on the share and writes nothing.</string>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog asks for this only when you play a stream whose address is on your own network, such as a server at home. Playing from the internet at large does not need it, and XPCog looks for nothing on your network by itself.</string>

	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog plays audio and never records any, so it does not ask for a microphone. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>An audio player has no use for a camera, and XPCog does not ask for one. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog has no use for your contacts and does not ask for them. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
	<key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog has no use for your calendar and does not ask for it. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
	<key>NSRemindersUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog has no use for your reminders and does not ask for them. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog has no use for your photo library and does not ask for it. Album art comes from the audio files themselves. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it.</string>
	<key>NSLocationUsageDescription</key>
	<string>XPCog has no use for your location and does not ask for it. If you are reading this in a permission alert, deny it: something is asking on XPCog's behalf that should not be.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
